     5	
     6	## Boundaries and contracts
     7	
     8	Triplex prepares one native SPARQL MODIFY containing its operation marker,
     9	assertions, claims, journal and metadata. A globally owned broker admits every
    10	native modification, including recursive scratch, rebuild and maintenance.
    11	QLever owns all facts and persisted snapshots. The broker's append-only intent
    12	journal records operation lifecycle, not a replacement fact database.
    13	
    14	`PreparedModify` ephemerally carries operation ID, attempt ID, immutable logical
    15	payload digest, one trusted adapter-generated MODIFY, and separate native marker,
    16	footprint and complete-proof queries. `RecoveryIntent` retains only identities,
    17	digests, expected count and bounded selectors. The request also has a native-attempt digest. Reusing one attempt
    18	with different bytes is an identity error. An explicit new attempt is allowed
    19	only after a previous attempt was proved not committed in a closed/reloaded
    20	epoch; the logical digest must match.
    21	
    22	`NativeWorker` provides fence verification, bounded MODIFY submission, complete
    23	marker/fact verification, process termination with closed-epoch proof, snapshot
    24	fsync, and restart/reload with a new epoch. The eventual owned container must
    25	require a broker-only access token and omit `--no-access-check`; client
    26	applications cannot receive the native update token. Every modifying route must
    27	use this broker. A process-local queue or per-scope lock cannot establish this.
    28	
    29	## Data flow and failure states
    30	
    31	1. Acquire `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX)` on a persistent global lock file. Never unlink
    32	   the lock file: different inode locks would break cross-process ownership.
    33	2. Read and validate the complete append-only journal. Torn, corrupt or
    34	   contradictory records hold the broker closed. Resolve any prior unfinished
    35	   operation before admitting another modification.
    36	3. Verify native ownership/token fencing, basename and port. Append the bounded recovery intent and
    37	   fsync the journal before network submission. Fsync the directory when
    38	   creating journal/lock files. Enforce bounded intent/journal sizes.
    39	4. Submit the single MODIFY. On any error, cancellation or uncertain response,
    40	   quarantine all writes while still holding the global lock. Do not inspect a
    41	   marker in the potentially poisoned mutable worker as proof of completion.
    42	5. Terminate the owned worker and verify its epoch is closed. Fsync the native
    43	   `.update-triples` file and containing directory, then restart from that file.
    44	   Never issue a following modification before this reload. Failure to stop,
    45	   sync, start or verify the reloaded state remains outcome unknown and closed.
    46	6. Resolve the complete marker and payload proof in the reloaded snapshot.
    47	   Present-and-complete means committed. Absent means not committed only with
    48	   this closed-epoch/reload proof. Partial or inconsistent state remains closed.
    49	7. On ordinary success, verify complete native proof, fsync the native snapshot
    50	   and its directory, then append/fsync the committed resolution before ACK.
    51	   A lost ACK can be resolved from the durable intent identity. Preserve all
    52	   journal records, snapshots, temporary files and native datasets.
    53	
    54	Queries may continue to read an old published QLever snapshot during normal
    55	modification. Quarantine/restart can make reads unavailable. The broker must not
    56	advertise zero downtime, distributed ownership, physical power-loss durability
    57	or transactional integration until those behaviors are separately demonstrated.
    58	
    59	## Model and fault-test packet to implement
    60	
    61	Use a file-backed model worker with distinct mutable, published and durable
    62	states. Model native failures before apply, after partial mutation, before
    63	writeback, after atomic rename before ACK, and after broker intent/commit fsync.
    64	Use actual child-process death (`os._exit`) to show a second broker recovers an
    65	unfinished intent before a next modification. Exercise two independent broker
    66	processes against the same kernel lock and prove their apply intervals never
    67	overlap. Include refusal to stop, snapshot fsync failure, torn journal, marker
    68	without complete payload, changed logical digest and direct-token bypass.
    69	
    70	After the isolated query-layout wave releases its containers, run native trials
    71	only on another copied owned QLever dataset, with token fencing and unchanged
    72	aggregate resource/disk admission. Native cancellation during mutation,
    73	unwritable snapshot publication and process death after rename before ACK are
    74	separate experiments. Verify whole marker/facts/claims/journal state, prior
    75	acknowledged state and post-reload source cuts. No model result qualifies these
    76	native fault cases or the full Triplex runtime integration.
