    94	- **Not covered by `-m`.** With `-m 1G` the server still grew past 3 GiB and died during a second 2.25M-triple insert (total delta 4.5M). The kernel message was not captured and Docker's `OOMKilled` flag was already set by an earlier index-build kill, so the OOM cause is inferred (no assertion text in the log, process gone). [P] Confidence Medium on cause, High on "memory growth is not bounded by `-m`".
    95	
    96	### 1.5 Evidence: `--persist-updates`
    97	
    98	- **What is stored.** After **every** request the server writes the full set of inserted and deleted triples (4 Ids each = 32 B, plus the local vocabulary) to `<base>.update-triples.tmp` and renames it over the old file (`src/index/DeltaTriples.cpp:648-672 @ 6c5861a`). It is a snapshot rewrite, not a log. Internal language-tag triples are regenerated at load. [S]
    99	- **Size** [P]: 93.8 MB for 1.0M triples with unique terms (94 B per triple), 72.1 MB for 2.25M triples with reused terms (32 B per triple).
   100	- **Replay at start** [P]: 2.25M triples in ~10.0 s, 4.5M triples in 22.6 s (~5 us per triple), RSS 1.87 GB after the 4.5M replay. A restart therefore costs proportional to the delta, and a delta that fit before a restart can fail to fit during replay.
   101	- **Durability** [S]: the writer is a buffered `FILE*` (`src/util/Serializer/FileSerializer.h:18-30 @ 6c5861a`). I found no `fsync` or `fdatasync` in the persistence path (grep over `src/util/Serializer`, `DeltaTriples.cpp`). Process-crash safe through rename, not power-loss safe. Confidence Medium.
   131	### 1.7 Evidence: compaction (`rebuild-index`)
   132	
   133	- **It exists and is online.** `curl -d cmd=rebuild-index` (access token unless `--no-access-check`) builds a new index from the current permutations plus delta, then atomically swaps it in. Queries and updates continue during the build. Updates that arrive meanwhile are carried over by a snapshot diff (`DeltaTriples::addFromSnapshotDiff`). [S] `src/engine/Server.cpp:567-580, 1635-1727 @ 6c5861a`, `src/libqlever/Qlever.cpp:754-815 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/IndexRebuilder.cpp:447-632 @ 6c5861a`. v0.6.0 release notes list "swap in the new index at runtime" (#2832) and the automatic strategy (#3163) [W] https://github.com/ad-freiburg/qlever/releases/tag/v0.6.0.
   134	- **No export and re-run of `qlever-index` is needed.** The docs say so and the algorithm is a vocabulary merge plus a streaming permutation rewrite. [W] https://docs.qlever.dev/rebuild-index/
   135	- **Automatic trigger.** `--rebuild-index-strategy automatic:min:max:fraction`, for example `automatic:10000:1000000:0.1`, fires when `inserted + deleted` reaches `clamp(fraction x index_triples, min, max)` (`src/engine/RebuildIndexStrategy.h:25-45 @ 6c5861a`, `src/ServerMain.cpp:168-177 @ 6c5861a`). Default is `manual`. [S]
   136	- **Working-directory trap** [P]. Without `cd` into the index directory the call returns HTTP 400 `The directory "rebuild.<ts>.tmp" is not a subdirectory of the directory of the current index`. The temp and previous directories must be relative and inside the index's own directory (`src/libqlever/Qlever.cpp:484-520 @ 6c5861a`). The repo's start script does `cd /data` with a relative base `triplex`, which satisfies this.
   137	- **Measured** [P] (2 CPUs):
   138	
   139	| Index | Triples rebuilt | Wall | Peak RSS | Notes |
   140	|-------|----------------|------|----------|-------|
   141	| P1: 465k base + 1.025M delta | 1.49M | 4.4 s | 1.54 GB | 3.6 s of it writing 2.2M vocabulary words (0.6M words/s) |
   142	| R: 11.94M base + 100k delta | 12.04M | 4.8 s | 1.33 GB | 3.6M words in 1.4 s, 6 permutations at ~23M triples/s. Scratch build of the same index: 35.2 s |
   143	
   144	- **Disk.** New index is built next to the old one. The old index moves to `previous.<build date>/`. Default `--rebuild-keep-previous-index-dirs` is `original-and-most-recent`, so disk use is 2x to 3x the index until pruned. After the swap the persisted delta file shrinks to 55 bytes. [S][P]
   145	- **What is lost.** Text index and loaded materialized views do not carry over (`Server.cpp:1655-1667 @ 6c5861a`). Irrelevant for the repo today. [S]
   166	**Probe B: a timeout during the mutation phase.** 1,500 subjects loaded, then `INSERT { GRAPH <Xi> { ?a <q> ?b } } WHERE { 1500 x 1500 cross join }` (2.25M triples) with a per-request `timeout`, then one trivial successful update, then four counts over the same graph through different permutations.
   167	
   168	| Timeout | HTTP | Count via `?s <q> ?o` (PSO/POS) | Count via `<y5> ?p ?o` (SPO) | Count via `?s ?p <y7>` (OPS) | Count via `?s ?p ?o` |
   169	|---------|------|------|------|------|------|
   170	| 400 ms to 2,000 ms | 429 "Operation timed out." | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
   171	| 2,500 ms | 429 | **2,250,000** | 0 | 0 | 0 |
   172	| 3,000 ms | 429 | **2,250,000** | 1,500 | 0 | 2,250,000 |
   173	| 4,000 ms | no reply (server died) | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a |
   174	
   175	A timed-out update (HTTP 429) was **partially applied, to some permutations only**, and the next successful update published that inconsistent state. The persisted file after restart contained exactly one complete 2.25M-triple set, so the junk was in memory only; a restart discarded it. Source explanation [S]: `locateAndAddTriples` loops over the six permutations and calls `throwIfCancelled()` between them (`DeltaTriples.cpp:200-225 @ 6c5861a`), while the hash sets are marked only afterwards (`DeltaTriples.cpp:476-478 @ 6c5861a`). Confidence High that it happens, Medium on every other consequence (for example I did not test whether a `rebuild-index` after such a state copies the junk into the new permutations).
   176	
   177	**Probe C: the server died inside an update, after the persisted file was written.** In the 4,000 ms run above and in the second 2.25M-triple insert of 1.4, the log shows `Done processing update`, then the process disappears with no error text, and the client gets no usable reply (HTTP 000 in the 4,000 ms run, a stale response file in the second insert). The persisted file already held the full update (`writeToDisk` runs before the snapshot deep copy, `DeltaTriples.cpp:536-541 @ 6c5861a`), so after the restart the update was present although the client never got a reply. A client must therefore resolve an unacknowledged write by reading a receipt (the repo's operation-receipt triple does exactly that), never by assuming it failed. [P] Confidence High on the sequence, Medium on the cause (memory exhaustion during the snapshot copy, not captured).
   178	
   179	**Version token and CAS.** There is no commit id. The repo's single `DELETE/INSERT WHERE` guarded by `FILTER NOT EXISTS` and a position triple (`QleverStorageAdapter.ts:215-230`) is a valid compare-and-swap in 0.6.0 because updates are serialized and the guard is evaluated against the live mutable state under the update thread. [S] Confidence Medium (I did not race it).
   180	
   181	### 1.9 Implications
   182	
   183	**For the projection design**
   184	1. Put the entity projection and the assertion history in separate named graphs, and require `FROM <urn:triplex:current>` (or inject it) for user SPARQL. Both graphs share one delta and one rebuild.
   185	2. Use one update request per Triplex transaction, never several operations in one request, because failed multi-op requests leak.
   186	3. After any update that returned an error or timeout, treat the server state as suspect until a restart. If the receipt triple is absent, do not just retry. Restart QLever first, then re-check the receipt.
   187	4. Coalesce writes. Per-request overhead is O(delta), so many small requests are quadratic. Prefer batches of thousands of triples.
   188	5. Every update clears the entire query result cache, so read-heavy workloads lose cache hits on every write.
   189	6. Avoid `CLEAR`, `DROP`, `MOVE` of big graphs. They become one delta delete per triple.
   190	
   191	**For 100M facts**
   192	- A fact write costs ~1 to 2 delta triples in the plain projection (insert plus delete of the old value), ~7 to 8 in a compact assertion graph, ~30 to 37 in the current reified layout. So 1M delta triples is ~110k facts (compact + plain) or ~33k facts (reified).
   193	- Budget 1M delta triples as the soft ceiling (0.4 to 1.7 GB at rest, up to 2x during an update, ~1.5 s per request, ~94 MB rewritten per request). Turn on `--rebuild-index-strategy automatic:<min>:1000000:0.01`-style limits. The right min and fraction depend on the base size and need an experiment (open question 1).
   441	## 8. Scale estimate: 100M facts on 6 CPUs and 12 GiB
   442	
   443	**Inputs** are the measured per-fact figures above (400k facts), extrapolated linearly [E]. Linear extrapolation is generous for vocabulary dedupe in P and optimistic for build rate at large sizes (external merges). Treat disk numbers as ±25% and times as ranges.
   444	
   445	| | **R** reified (current) | **C** compact assertion | **P** plain projection | **P + C** (proposed) |
   446	|---|---|---|---|---|
   447	| Triples at 100M facts | ~2.99 B (29.9 per fact) | ~702 M (7.0) | ~118 M (1.18) | ~820 M |
   448	| Index disk (permutations + vocabulary) | ~72 GB | ~13.8 GB | ~3.0 GB | ~16.8 GB |
   449	| Vocabulary words | ~860 M | ~157 M | ~36 M | ~193 M |
   450	| Disk during a build or rebuild (old + new + `previous.*` kept) | 150 to 220 GB | 28 to 42 GB | 6 to 9 GB | 34 to 51 GB |
   451	| Scratch build, measured rate at 2 CPUs | 2.4 h | 26 min | 6 min | ~32 min |
   452	| Scratch build, est. at 6 CPUs (1.5 to 2.5x) | 1.0 to 1.6 h | 10 to 17 min | 2.5 to 4 min | 13 to 21 min |
   453	| `rebuild-index` at 2 CPUs measured rate (2.5M triples/s on R, vocabulary-bound for new words) | ~20 min | ~5 min | ~1 to 2 min | ~6 to 7 min |
   454	| Delta triples per fact written | ~30 to 37 | ~8 | ~1 to 2 | ~9 to 10 |
   455	| Facts written before 1M delta triples | ~30k | ~125k | ~500k | ~100k |
   456	| Idle server RSS | 46 MB at 12M triples, larger indexes untested | | | |
   457	| Hot working set vs the 12 GiB VM | far larger than RAM | 14 GB, about RAM | fits | 17 GB, partly cached |
   458	
   459	**Reading the table.**
   460	- **The reified layout's triple count is the cost driver.** It is 30x P and 4x C in triples, 24x P and 5x C in bytes. At 3 billion triples it exceeds the 12 GiB VM for any realistic hot set.
   461	- **P + C is ~1/4 of R by triples and by bytes.** Index sizes of ~17 GB sit just above the VM's RAM, so queries on cold regions read from disk. Real latency at that scale is an open question (7.2 caveat).
   462	- **Rebuild cadence for P + C:** a fact write adds ~9 to 10 delta triples. With a 1M delta ceiling that is a rebuild every ~100k fact writes. At 10 facts per second that is every ~2.8 hours. Each rebuild takes ~6 to 7 minutes at the measured 2-CPU rate, scaled to P + C. The default rebuild thread settings are deliberately low, so 6 CPUs may not shorten it much. The old index keeps serving meanwhile (CPU and disk are shared).
   463	- **Further savings, not measured here:** `--only-pso-and-pos-permutations` drops four permutations for the history graph if it is never queried by variable predicate (expected disk -60%, build faster, but one flag per index, so it needs its own server or a rule that raw users must not hit it). `--encode-as-id` (IRIs with a numeric tail become ids, no vocabulary entry, ordering between encoded and plain IRIs is wrong) only helps entity IRIs of the form `<prefix><digits>`. `--iri-as-blank-node-regexes` is experimental.
   464	- **Build memory:** set `num-triples-per-batch` to 1M or lower and `--stxxl-memory` 512M to 1G on the 12 GiB VM. R's default settings needed >3 GB at 12M triples, P+C at 100M facts are ~10x lower in triples per fact but the same batching applies.
    29	## Native surfaces and invariants
    30	
    31	The public TypeScript schema model remains Triplex: Attribute/EntityType,
    32	TypeSchema, ConfigStore releases, branded database scopes and declared native
    33	parameter/result contracts. Native means authoring XTQL/SQL/SPARQL instead of
    34	a Datalog AST; it does not permit losing exact values, temporal cuts, assertion
    35	identity, history or command/receipt semantics.
    36	
    37	| Path | Owning source | Boundary |
    38	| --- | --- | --- |
    39	| XTDB XTQL | [NativeQueries.ts](../../packages/xtdb/src/native/NativeQueries.ts), [Sources.ts](../../packages/xtdb/src/native/Sources.ts) | `makeXtdbNativeQueries(...).select(...)` uses XTQL. `selectSql(...)` is the SQL alternative. Both use declared contracts, one captured upper commit P and public R/V selectors. Qualify both independently; existing SQL recipes do not prove arbitrary XTQL parity. |
    40	| QLever SPARQL | [NativeQueries.ts](../../packages/qlever/src/native/NativeQueries.ts), [NativeContext.ts](../../packages/qlever/src/native/NativeContext.ts), [BasisRelations.ts](../../packages/qlever/src/native/BasisRelations.ts) | Schema-aware closed facts/history sources, scoped RDF assertion identities and captured logical cut. Raw SPARQL numeric behavior is narrower than the full Triplex value domain; use the declared mapping and retain unsupported cases. |
    41	| Shared write compatibility | [XTDB adapter](../../packages/xtdb/src/XtdbStorageAdapter.ts), [QLever adapter](../../packages/qlever/src/QleverStorageAdapter.ts), [Core NativeQuery](../../packages/core/src/native/NativeQuery.ts) | Preserve atomic command claims, ordered commit positions, causal journal, pending overlays, retries and unknown-outcome reconciliation. A successful read does not qualify writes, Datalog or an entire native language. |
    42	
    43	Ordinary `TemporalBasis` accepts `recordedAt`/`validAt`; exact P is internal to
    44	stable pagination or explicit in the **native** read contract. Do not inject
    45	private P into ordinary reads. Recorded time is not XTDB engine system time.
    46	Retain half-open/null interval boundaries, same-timestamp distinct commits,
    47	fixed-cut continuation and reopen. Ordinary raw assertion-lifetime metadata is
    48	a compatibility contract distinct from the new as-of snapshot-assertion target.
   123	The full ten-goal target remains open: exact public types/schemas, native query
   124	semantics, coherent negation/aggregates, lifecycle/resource safety, recovery,
   125	maintenance, history/presence/deletion/cascades, and public SDK integration at
   126	**10m distinct entities /100m representative active domain facts**, with sparse
   127	and representative profiles, history and write amplification counted separately.
   128	See [goals](../../wiki/goals.md). No Rust rewrite is justified without a measured
   129	TypeScript bottleneck. Preserve failed attempts and immutable histories; changes
   130	need new named source/build/fixture identities and fresh independent evidence.

